            <ArticleTitle>Classification and risk stratification of patients with acute chest pain using a low discriminatory level of cardiac troponin T.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>130-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Cardiac troponins are the biochemical markers of choice for the evaluation of acute coronary syndromes (ACS). Using the first-generation test, most studies related adverse outcome to &gt; 0.20 or 0.10 microg/l cardiac troponin T (cTnT) levels. With the highly sensitive and specific second- and third-generation assays, cTnT is undetectable in most healthy individuals.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="HYPOTHESIS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We evaluated whether a lower cTnT level, within 24 h of admission, could indicate an increased risk of future complications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">During 1998-1999, clinical data were collected in 260 patients with ACS. Cardiac troponin T was measured at arrival, and 4, 8, and 12-24 h thereafter. The maximum cTnT value was then used to assess, over a 15-month follow-up period, the cumulative risk of death or myocardial infarction (MI), as well as rates of events according to quartiles of cTnT values.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Patients with &lt; or = 0.03 microg/l cTnT levels had the lowest rate of adverse events and the best Kaplan-Meier event-free survival curve. Increasing cTnT levels were associated with stepwise increases in mortality rates and with a constant 10-fold increase in MI rates during follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A low threshold cTnT elevation is recommended to assess the risk of ACS. All cTnT elevations &gt; 0.03 microg/l predict a higher risk of MI during follow-up, whereas increasing values predict mortality in relation to the amount of elevation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
